Docker ENTRYPOINT Example

This project demonstrates how Docker ENTRYPOINT and CMD work together. The Dockerfile uses the debian:trixie-slim base image, installs Vim, creates a working directory /eline, copies the entrypoint.sh script, makes it executable, and sets it as the container's ENTRYPOINT. The script accepts a command-line argument, stores it in the variable NAME, and prints it using echo.

Dockerfile:

FROM debian:trixie-slim
RUN apt update && apt upgrade -y && apt install vim -y
WORKDIR /eline
COPY entrypoint.sh .
RUN chmod +x /eline/entrypoint.sh
ENTRYPOINT ["/eline/entrypoint.sh"]
CMD ["name"]

Entrypoint Script:

#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -e
NAME="$1"
echo "The name is $NAME"

When the container is started using:

docker run myimage

Docker executes:

/eline/entrypoint.sh name

and the output is:

The name is name

If a custom argument is supplied:

docker run myimage Abhima

Docker executes:

/eline/entrypoint.sh Abhima

and the output becomes:

The name is Abhima

This example shows that ENTRYPOINT defines the executable that always runs, while CMD provides default arguments that can be overridden when starting the container.
